The assertions (assume and assert sections) provide proof that the LastIndexOfContract maintains the data structure's correctness property while modifying LastIndexOf method (x). It does so by comparing the old array index of the element with the new array's index of the element for elements that are present in the old array. If an element is not present in the old array, it checks that the new list does not contain the element.

The assertions provide a formal verification of the LastIndexOfContract specification. They verify that the contract is preserved or not while there is a change in the method.

The assumptions make use of the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute, which allows Pex to substitute the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute method under test (including its parameters) with fresh values in method calls in test method (PUT_LastIndexOfContract). It does so, Pex generates an alternative paths of test for each method.

The observers used in the PexObserve namespace provide a simple way to see what values are being observed to aid in debugging. You can observe any public get-only properties of any parameter you want to test, in this case, arrList and x. 

The assertions within the Assert namespace are used to verify the preservation of data structure property and the correctness of LastIndexOf method.

This is a sample of using Pex on part of the .NET ArrayList class for unit testing. This example uses LastIndexOf and other properties. It can be used with any other data structure and its methods for the unit testing.

Lastly, remember to modify the class and its methods and properties to pass the requirements and add the necessary asserions based on the contract of the method, the data structure it modifies and the specification of the method. 

Keep in mind that your actual code may have different or more complex conditions and constraints so you'll have to adjust this sample accordingly when you use it in your own testing code.

The important part of all of this is the PexContract attribute, and the various other attributes it might work with, such as PexCustomization, PexAllowSpecialCharacters and so on. They offer many different ways of controlling Pex's behavior and can be quite customizable. You should check out the MSDN reference for more details on how to use them.

NOTE: ArrayList and similar classes are not designed for concurrent access from multiple threads. For such scenarios, you could consider using thread-safe alternatives like ConcurrentBag, ConcurrentQueue, ConcurrentStack, etc.

NOTE: Remember to run the code with permissions that allow it to create and delete directories. Some unit test frameworks may need to do this to run your tests.

NOTE: If you use Pex with NUnit tests, you may encounter issues with automatic discovery of test methods.

NOTE: The test methods of your unit tests should be void and they should have at least one parameter of the type you want to test. The parameters should have the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute applied to them. To use Pex with a static method, the unit test method should have a parameter of the static method's own class type, and the static method itself should have the PexStaticMember attribute applied to it.

NOTE: Pex requires your assembly to be signed. This is a requirement by the CLR to ensure method pointers are preserved correctly.

The unit test method should be public and it should have at least one parameter of the type you want to test. The parameters should have the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute applied to them.

For example:

public partial class MyArrayListTest 
{
    [Test]
    public void LastIndexOf_Test([PexAssumeUnderTest]MyArrayList list, int item)
    {
        // test code here
    } 
}

