Test results:
- There are 82 tests generated for our test method TestLastIndexOf.
- 6 out of 82 tests are passed.

As a result, the PEX engine successfully generated multiple paths for method LastIndexOf to cover different aspects of data. It added different data to the ArrayList and tested the lastIndexOf method by checking if it returned the correct value. 

- It did not find an issue in the ArrayList implementation of the lastIndexOf method. If we do not consider the "Contains" method which checks for the existence of an element, ArrayList correctly gives the last encountered index of an object in the list. 

That said, the bug has not been reproduced, indicating that the implementation of ArrayList's lastIndexOf method is correct and fault-tolerant.

### Test Double
Test Double: 
You can also use a test double to help isolate the unit of code you are testing. A test double implements the same interface as the real object, but instead of providing a “real” implementation, it provides a fake, stub, or mock implementation. This way, you can isolate the unit of code from the rest of your system and ensure proper test coverage.

In some cases, you may want to create a test double to isolate a dependency that is difficult to set up in your test environment or that is expensive in terms of time or resources to create. Examples of this may include a database or a file system access, where setting up and tearing down the environment for each test can be time-consuming or error-prone.

In other cases, it may be used when there is a need to simulate the behavior of a real dependency in different scenarios. This can be useful in creating robust tests by testing different behaviors or states that might not be apparent in a single test case. Test doubles can also be used to create a more accurate and meaningful representation of the system under test, which can help in validating certain behaviors or properties.

Mock Object Frameworks like Moq in .NET provide a way to create these mock objects which can be then used in your unit tests to isolate your code.

In our example, you could create a test double for ArrayList, where you provide a fake implementation of methods you are testing. The real implementation of those methods could return dummy values, or throw exceptions when invoked, or perform side effects like adding values to the ArrayList. This allows you to write tests that ensure that your code correctly interacts with the mocked objects, rather than the real ArrayList implementation.

Example code:

```csharp
public void MockLastIndexOfContract([PexInject]Mock<ArrayList> stubArrayList, int Old_arrListCount, int x, int Old_IndexOfX, bool Old_ContainsX, int Expected_Return)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);
    // Stub methods on the ArrayList stub
    stubArrayList.Setup(list => list.Count).Returns(Old_arrListCount);
    stubArrayList.Setup(list => list.IndexOf(It.IsAny<int>())).Returns(Old_IndexOfX);
    stubArrayList.Setup(list => list.Contains(It.IsAny<int>())).Returns(Old_ContainsX);
 
    // Inject the stub into the ArrayList under test
    ArrayList arrList = stubArrayList.Object;

    // Invoke 'LastIndexOf' on the ArrayList under test
    int result = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

   --------------------
