If you run the "LastIndexOfPartialTest_ArrayList_x" method, it can perform various test cases for the LastIndexOf method in ArrayList, and it will fail on one of the tests where ArrayList input [1,2,3,1] and x = 2 should return 1.

The reason for the failure can be the lack of code under the last precondition, which is a bug and should be resolved.

If PexMethodUnderTest is the method you want to test, you can change it to the method you want to test and the test will rerun. The test will fail when it expects an exception to be thrown but it actually doesn't - when LastIndexOf() returns an illegal index.

Note: The current bug that pex generates doesn't account for the IndexOf returning -1 when a non-existing element (x) is queried. In a full-fledged ArrayList implementation, this is a logical fallacy. 

This test method can only be used as a part of a larger testing approach for verifying the behavior of LastIndexOf(). An additional property or action that involves non-existing elements is required for greater test coverage.

```java

public int LastIndexOf(int item)
{
  for (int i = Count - 1; i >= 0; i--)
    if (Array[i] == item)
      return i;
  return -1;
}

```

ArrayList's LastIndexOf() method implementation might look like this. This method starts at the end of the ArrayList and travels backwards. If it finds the item, it returns the current index. If it doesn't find the item, it returns -1. That means if you ask for the last index of an item in an empty array, you'll get -1, which is the proper return value and makes the error message more clear.

Ideally, you should create a property or an action that is related to the LastIndexOf(). E.g., adding an item and asking for its last index. That will validate the behavior of the LastIndexOf(). The current test seems to be checking something else, not what the user is likely to use the LastIndexOf() for. The test should be designed as a regular part of a larger testing strategy.

